t is is done  traditionally maybe I'm just a renegade  or something if I see  you know I get occasionally emails from  teasel teachers of English to speakers  of other languages that's the name of  their organization teachers of English  to speakers of other languages that's a  special organization and they have  seminars and they have books and they  talk about things that most of them have  probably heard many many times before  they kind of reinforce all of their I  wouldn't say prejudices but all of their  established views they're probably  closely connected with the Center for  Applied linguistics and they're all out  lobbying for more money and the amount  of money that is spent on these  organizations it's a lot of fun it's  great you know in the end what comes out  of that a lot of redundant textbooks  redundant you know exercises and stuff  when really all we need is the language  if we can get people producing content  talking in simple terms about their  lives for example getting it up  somewhere in audio and text for people  to listen to and in some systems such as  we have a link to allow people to  navigate it although ours can improve  we're working on improving it so that  people can find content a of interest  and be at their level and some means for  them to go through and you know pick out  the words they don't know and learn them  and that's all and then when they're  ready to speak have some people  available that they can speak to I don't  think it's more difficult I don't think  it's much more difficult than that I  really think that you can take all  language learning and reduce it down  essentially to what I have said in other  words gradually sort of a range of  content starting from very short bits 30  seconds one minute hello today I got up  I had breakfast with my husband and  children and we went to school that's  what I did today one listen to it 1015  times and gradually going from that  eventually hitting conversations in  conversations the vocabulary used is not  very difficult so that's good for a sort  of an intermediate learner they're  picking up on the intonation the rhythm  of the language and then you move to  more  more difficult content eventually into  specialized content professional content  literature you know whatever and along  the way when you're ready you can talk  to someone and if you make some mistakes  they'll explain it to you but you won't  be required to remember the explanation  because you're going to forget it anyway  and then they'll explain it again and  again and eventually you'll get used to  language I think it all boils down to  that I'm as usual perhaps a little bit  extreme I look forward to hearing  people's views thank you
 

